# Build context for backend/Dockerfile, which builds from the repository root
# because the app reads data/catalog.json from there.
#
# The first block is not an optimisation. A Docker build context is uploaded
# whole, and anything in it can be COPYd into a layer that ships. Keeping .env
# and *.db out of the context means a credential cannot reach a deployed image
# even by mistake — the same promise ci.yml enforces about the tree.
.env
.env.*
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.db
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3

# Version control and CI
.git
.gitignore
.github

# Frontend — the frontend image builds from frontend/ with its own context
frontend

# Python and test artefacts
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache
.hypothesis
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
.venv
venv

# Node
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Local planning and working notes: large, private, and irrelevant to a running
# container, and untracked anyway. Excluded so they never enter a build context.
*.md
!README.md
docs
